    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah.jpg" alt="Prithvi Narayan Shah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Prithvi_Narayan_Shah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the Record, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> was created by the conquests of the small hill state of Gorkha, west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a>, who became king of Gorkha in 1743, set out to bring under his rule the many principalities of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a>, among them the western confederations known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaubisi-rajya">Chaubisi</a> and Baisi kingdoms and the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newar</a> kingdoms of the valley: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, Lalitpur (Patan) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>. He spent over two decades encircling the valley, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuwakot">Nuwakot</a> in 1744 and imposing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> that cut its trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. After two failed assaults he took the hill town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtipur">Kirtipur</a> in 1766, and in September 1768 his troops entered Kathmandu during the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-jatra">Indra Jatra</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a> fell soon afterwards and Bhaktapur in 1769, and Prithvi Narayan Shah moved his capital to Kathmandu. A small British expedition under Captain George Kinloch, sent in 1767 to help the Newar king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaya-prakash-malla">Jaya Prakash Malla</a>, had already failed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> lowlands. In his political testament, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divya-upadesh">Divya Upadesh</a></em>, Prithvi Narayan Shah described his kingdom as ‘a yam between two boulders’, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and urged caution towards both. His successors and regents, notably his younger son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-of-nepal">Bahadur Shah</a>, continued the expansion after his death in 1775.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kathmandu.jpg" alt="Kathmandu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kathmandu-Durbar_Square-06-Mahavishnu-Kuh-Vishnu-Pratapamalla-Jagannath-2007-gje.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gerd Eichmann, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> was overwhelmingly a society of subsistence farmers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> was grown in the valleys and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and other crops sustained the hill villages. Much of the land was granted as <em>birta</em> to nobles, priests and favourites or as <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a></em> in lieu of salary to soldiers and officials, and peasants owed rents and unpaid labour. The state was officially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, the king being seen as protector of cows and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> hierarchy was enforced by law: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muluki-ain">Muluki Ain of 1854</a> ranked all groups, from the sacred-thread-wearing high castes through the ‘liquor-drinking’ castes, which included many hill peoples, down to untouchable castes, and prescribed different punishments according to caste. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newar</a> cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> preserved a rich urban culture of Hindu and Buddhist festivals, crafts and architecture. Trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, Newar merchants kept houses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, and Nepal minted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins that circulated in Tibet until disputes over debased coinage helped spark war in 1788. From the nineteenth century, pay and pensions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkha</a> soldiers in British service became important to hill economies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was abolished in 1924. After 1951 the country opened to foreign aid and to tourism, especially trekking and mountaineering.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nalapani"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-nalapani.jpg" alt="Battle of Nalapani" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nalapani">Battle of Nalapani</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Death_of_Rollo_Gillespie_(Cassell%27s_illustrated_history_of_India).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Henry Thompson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Gorkhali army that created the kingdom was a disciplined force of hill infantry, armed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, swords and the curved <em>khukuri</em> knife and organised in companies partly on the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>’s sepoys. Its conquests were rapid: by the early nineteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> stretched along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teesta-river">Teesta</a> river in the east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> in the west, having absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> in 1790 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> in 1803–1804, and Nepali forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amar-singh-thapa">Amar Singh Thapa</a> besieged Kangra from 1806 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> intervention in 1809. Expansion northwards provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-nepalese-war">Sino-Nepalese War</a> of 1788–1792; in 1792 a Qing army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukanggan">Fuk’anggan</a> advanced close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> and forced Nepal to accept peace and send regular tribute missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Expansion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> of 1814–1816. Nepali troops inflicted heavy losses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nalapani">Kalunga</a>, but British forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ochterlony">David Ochterlony</a> eventually prevailed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sugauli">Treaty of Sugauli</a> cost Nepal about a third of its territory. Impressed by their opponents, the British began recruiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkha</a> soldiers, who later served in both world wars. Nepal also fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in 1855–1856, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-bahadur-rana">Jang Bahadur Rana</a> sent troops to help the British suppress the Indian rebellion of 1857.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bhimsen-thapa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhimsen-thapa.jpg" alt="Bhimsen Thapa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bhimsen-thapa">Bhimsen Thapa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhimsen_Thapa_sitting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anand Muni Shakya, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Shah kings ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> monarchs, but real power often lay with regents, queens or chief ministers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhimsen-thapa">Bhimsen Thapa</a> dominated the government as <em>mukhtiyar</em> from 1806 to 1837, and the struggles between noble factions culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kot-massacre">Kot massacre</a> of September 1846, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-bahadur-rana">Jang Bahadur Kunwar</a> and his brothers killed dozens of leading courtiers. Jang Bahadur, who took the family name Rana, became prime minister and in 1856 obtained a royal decree making the office hereditary within his family, with succession passing among brothers and cousins. For over a century the Shah kings were figureheads while the Rana prime ministers controlled the army, revenue and foreign relations, governing an isolated, conservative state through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muluki-ain">Muluki Ain</a> and personal decrees. The Ranas’ alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>’s independence, recognised formally in a treaty of 1923. In 1950–1951 King Tribhuvan, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepali-congress">Nepali Congress</a> party, brought Rana rule to an end. After a brief experiment with parliamentary government following the elections of 1959, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendra-of-nepal">King Mahendra</a> dismissed the cabinet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-p-koirala">B.P. Koirala</a> in December 1960 and introduced the partyless panchayat system under the constitution of 1962, which concentrated power in the palace until 1990. The constitution of 1990 then made Nepal a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with multiparty democracy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khas-people"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khas-people.jpg" alt="Khas people" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khas-people">Khas people</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deuda_dancers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Khaptad Digital Music, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> created the modern country of Nepal, uniting peoples of many languages, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> and religions under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> monarchy and spreading the Nepali (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khas-people">Khas</a>) language as the language of administration and wider communication. It was one of very few Asian states never colonised by a European power, preserving its independence as a buffer between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> through a careful policy of alliance and isolation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkha</a> soldiers recruited from its hills became a lasting part of the British and Indian armies, and their service in the two world wars made Nepal widely known abroad. The kingdom’s rulers patronised the temples and festivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, whose palace squares and shrines, inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1979, reflect both Malla and Shah building. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muluki-ain">Muluki Ain</a> shaped social relations long after its replacement in 1963, and the debates about caste, ethnicity and the Hindu state that it framed remain central in Nepali politics. Nepal was the world’s only officially Hindu kingdom until it was declared secular in 2006–2007, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> is still widely honoured as the founder of the nation. The kingdom’s mountains also made it a centre of world mountaineering after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-everest">Everest</a> was first climbed in 1953.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal.jpg" alt="Birendra of Nepal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal">Birendra of Nepal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birendra_Bir_Bikram_Shah_c._1967_(restoration).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CAPTAIN MEDUSA, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The monarchy’s end came through a series of popular movements. The first <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1990-peoples-movement">Jana Andolan</a></em> (People’s Movement) of 1990 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal">King Birendra</a> to lift the ban on political parties and accept a constitution making him a constitutional monarch. Parliamentary politics, however, was unstable, and in 1996 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-nepal-maoist-centre">Communist Party of Nepal (Maoist)</a> launched an armed insurgency that spread across rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and cost more than 13,000 lives by 2006. On 1 June 2001 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dipendra-of-nepal">Crown Prince Dipendra</a> shot dead King Birendra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aishwarya-of-nepal">Queen Aishwarya</a> and other members of the royal family at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narayanhiti-palace">Narayanhiti Palace</a> before dying of his own wounds, and Birendra’s brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyanendra-of-nepal">Gyanendra</a> became king. Gyanendra dismissed elected governments and on 1 February 2005 assumed direct power, suspending civil liberties. In April 2006 a second People’s Movement, uniting the parliamentary parties and the Maoists, brought weeks of mass protests and forced him to restore parliament, which stripped the crown of its powers. A Comprehensive Peace Accord in November 2006 ended the insurgency, and an interim constitution in 2007 declared Nepal a secular state. On 28 May 2008 the newly elected Constituent Assembly abolished the monarchy and proclaimed the Federal Democratic Republic of Nepal. Gyanendra left the royal palace, which became a museum, and a new federal constitution was promulgated in 2015.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a>’s conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> in 1768–1769, united the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> under the Shah dynasty, kept its independence between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and lasted until the Constituent Assembly abolished the monarchy in 2008.</em></p>

